Given:
ElevatedButton(
onPressed: () {
Navigator.push(
context,
MaterialPageRoute(builder: (_) => SecondPage()),
);
},
child: Text('Go to Second Page'),
)
According to the lecture's explanation of why this original code doesn't run directly on DartPad, what is the main cause?

Given this CounterPage code using `setState`:
class _CounterPageState extends State<CounterPage> {
int count = 0;
void increment() {
setState(() { count++; });
}
...
}
If replaced with just `count++;` WITHOUT calling `setState()`, what happens?

Given this `InheritedWidget` code:
class AppData extends InheritedWidget {
final String theme;
const AppData({required this.theme, required Widget child, Key? key})
: super(key: key, child: child);
static AppData of(BuildContext context) =>
context.dependOnInheritedWidgetOfExactType<AppData>()!;
}
What is the main purpose of `InheritedWidget` in this example?

Given this Provider code:
class CounterModel with ChangeNotifier {
int count = 0;
void increment() { count++; notifyListeners(); }
}
If the `notifyListeners();` line is removed from `increment()`, what happens?

In this code combining Navigation and State:
if (appState.loggedIn)
Navigator.pushNamed(context, '/dashboard');
else
Navigator.pushNamed(context, '/login');
What principle does this code illustrate?

In Navigator 2.0, which component's role does the following code illustrate?
class AppRouterDelegate extends RouterDelegate<AppRoute>
with ChangeNotifier, PopNavigatorRouterDelegateMixin<AppRoute> {
@override
Widget build(BuildContext context) {
return Navigator(
pages: [MaterialPage(child: HomePage())],
onPopPage: (route, result) => route.didPop(result),
);
}
}

In the "Managing App State" section with `ChangeNotifier`:
class AppState extends ChangeNotifier {
bool loggedIn = false;
void login() { loggedIn = true; notifyListeners(); }
}
Why is separating state (login/logout) into its own class away from the UI considered good practice (per the instructor's note)?

Given:
Widget build(BuildContext context) {
final w = MediaQuery.of(context).size.width;
return Scaffold(
body: Center(
child: Text(w < 600 ? "Mobile Layout" : "Tablet Layout"),
),
);
}
When the device is rotated from portrait to landscape, making the width exceed 600px, what happens to the displayed text?

Given:
LayoutBuilder(
builder: (context, c) {
return c.maxWidth > 500 ? WideCard() : NarrowCard();
},
);
If this `LayoutBuilder` is placed inside a `Container` with a fixed width of 300px (while the device screen is 800px wide), which widget is displayed?

Given this GridView code:
GridView.count(
crossAxisCount: width < 600 ? 2 : 4,
);
If the `width` variable is read ONCE in `initState()` instead of in `build()` (from MediaQuery), what problem could arise when the user rotates the screen afterward?

In the Responsive Movie Card example:
LayoutBuilder(
builder: (context, c) {
final isWide = c.maxWidth > 600;
return Row(children: [
Image.network(poster, width: isWide ? 150 : 100),
SizedBox(width: 16),
Expanded(child: Text(title))
]);
},
)
What is the role of `Expanded` wrapping `Text(title)` in this Row?

Given:
if (width < 600) return MobileView();
return TabletView();
If this breakpoint logic is placed in the `build()` function of a deeply nested child widget inside multiple constrained `Padding`/`Container` layers, but `width` is taken from `MediaQuery.of(context).size.width` (full screen width) instead of `LayoutBuilder`, what is the potential consequence?
